### 4.1.1 Phase 1: Requirement Analysis and Dataset Selection

The preliminary phase of the research methodology was concentrated on developing a thorough understanding of the problem space and identifying appropriate datasets to support the training and evaluation of the proposed system. The selection criteria were designed to ensure that the chosen datasets reflect the diversity and complexity of real-world crowd monitoring scenarios.

Based on the deployment constraints and the scope of the problem, a number of system requirements were established. The proposed system must be capable of producing accurate density estimates across a wide range of crowd densities, including sparse scenarios with fewer than 100 people and extreme-density scenarios exceeding 5,000. Beyond accuracy, the system must be computationally efficient, enabling inference at a throughput that is compatible with edge hardware such as the NVIDIA Jetson Nano. Privacy-preserving operation requires that all processing be performed on-device, without transmitting raw video frames to centralised servers. Furthermore, the architecture must be modular and scalable, permitting deployment across networks of cameras without prohibitive increases in computational overhead.

The selection of datasets was governed by the requirement that they collectively represent the full range of crowd density conditions encountered in practice. The following criteria were applied: a dataset must exhibit diversity in crowd density, spanning both sparse and highly congested scenes; point-level annotations must be of sufficient quality to support accurate density map generation; the dataset must provide enough training samples to ensure model convergence; and the dataset must be publicly available to enable reproducibility and direct comparison with published methods.

**Selected Datasets:**
- **NWPU-Crowd [Wang et al., 2020]:** 5,109 images, 2,133,375 head annotations, crowd counts ranging from 0 to 20,033 per image. Selected as the primary training and evaluation benchmark for all three deployed model components. Diverse in scene type, lighting condition, and camera perspective.
- **ShanghaiTech Part A [Zhang et al., 2016]:** 482 images (300 train, 182 test), 241,677 annotations, crowd counts ranging from 33 to 3,139. Used exclusively for cross-dataset generalisation evaluation in this work.
- **ShanghaiTech Part B [Zhang et al., 2016]:** 716 images (400 train, 316 test), 88,488 annotations, crowd counts ranging from 9 to 578. Also reserved for cross-dataset generalisation evaluation.

No samples from ShanghaiTech Part A or Part B are used in any stage of training, validation, or hyperparameter selection; these datasets serve exclusively to evaluate zero-shot generalisation.

---

### 4.1.2 Phase 2: Architecture Design and Model Selection

Following the requirements analysis, the architecture design phase addressed the fundamental accuracy-efficiency trade-off in crowd density estimation by introducing a hybrid architecture capable of adaptive model selection based on the complexity of the input scene.

**Architectural Reasoning:**

The central challenge of crowd density estimation is balancing accuracy against computational efficiency. Lightweight models support rapid inference but exhibit insufficient representational capacity for highly congested scenes. Computationally intensive models provide high accuracy on dense scenes but are impractical for edge deployment and incur unnecessary overhead on sparse inputs. Conventional approaches resolve this tension by pre-selecting a single model, leading to sub-optimal performance across the full density spectrum. The proposed hybrid architecture overcomes this limitation by dynamically selecting between two specialised lightweight models — one optimised for sparse scenes and one for dense scenes — based on a learned assessment of scene complexity.

**Architectural Components:**

**Component 1: LCDNet (Lightweight Crowd Density Network).** LCDNet is the efficiency-optimised element of the hybrid system, designed for sparse-to-medium density scenes (ground-truth count ≤ 100). The architecture employs an encoder-decoder topology with depthwise separable convolutions, which achieve an approximately 8× reduction in parameter count relative to standard convolutional architectures of equivalent representational depth, while maintaining expressive capability. Skip connections link corresponding encoder and decoder stages, preserving fine-grained spatial information across the downsampling hierarchy. LCDNet produces full-resolution density maps at the same spatial dimensions as the input image.

**Component 2: MobileCount (Deployed Dense Specialist — Knowledge-Distilled).** MobileCount is the accuracy-oriented element of the hybrid system, designed for dense crowd scenes (ground-truth count > 100). It employs the first 13 feature layers of a pretrained MobileNetV2 backbone as the encoder, providing computationally efficient hierarchical feature extraction that generalises well due to ImageNet pretraining. Three dilated convolution context blocks (dilation rate = 2) extract multi-scale contextual information without reducing spatial resolution. A skip-connection fusion module combines encoder representations at two resolution scales to improve density map accuracy. The output is a density map at 1/8 of the input resolution; the count is derived by spatial summation.

MobileCount is deployed in a **knowledge-distilled** form. Prior to deployment, it is trained to match the density map outputs of a high-capacity CSRNet teacher through the knowledge distillation procedure described in Phase 4.5. The teacher is discarded after distillation and is never included in the deployed pipeline.

**Component 3: CSRNet (Knowledge Distillation Teacher — Not Deployed).** CSRNet is used exclusively as a training-time teacher in the knowledge distillation phase. It consists of the first ten convolutional layers of VGG-16 as a pretrained frontend and six dilated convolutional layers (dilation rates: 2, 2, 2, 4, 4, 4) as the backend density estimator. Its 16.26M parameters make it unsuitable for direct edge deployment; however, its superior density estimation capability on dense scenes is transferred to the lightweight MobileCount student.

**Component 4: MobileNetV2 Routing Classifier.** The routing classifier uses a pretrained MobileNetV2 backbone with a custom two-class fully connected head to classify each input as sparse (count ≤ 100) or dense (count > 100). The routing threshold T = 100 was established on the basis of the NWPU-Crowd density distribution and is consistent with established crowd counting literature distinguishing sparse and dense crowd configurations.

---

### 4.1.3 Phase 3: Preprocessing Pipeline

This phase implements the complete data preprocessing pipeline that transforms raw annotated imagery into standardised training representations through a systematic multi-stage process involving density map generation, data augmentation, and normalisation.

**Stage 1: Density Map Generation:**

Ground-truth continuous density maps are synthesised from discrete head point annotations by placing adaptive Gaussian kernels at each annotated head position. The standard deviation σᵢ of the kernel at annotation i is computed as:

σᵢ = β · d̄ᵢ

where d̄ᵢ is the mean Euclidean distance from head position i to its k = 3 nearest neighbours within the same image, and β = 0.3 is the empirically calibrated scaling coefficient. This adaptive formulation ensures that kernel widths are proportional to local crowd density: narrower kernels in congested regions preserve spatial precision, while wider kernels in sparse regions provide adequate spatial coverage. Mass conservation is enforced throughout: ∫∫ D(x, y) dxdy = N, where N is the total number of annotated heads in the image, enabling ground-truth supervision by pixel-wise regression.

When an image contains only a single annotated person — making nearest-neighbour distance computation undefined — a fixed fallback sigma of 15.0 pixels is applied. All computed sigma values are clipped to the range [4.0, 30.0] pixels to prevent degenerate kernels from producing numerically unstable training signals.

**Stage 2: Data Augmentation:**

To improve model generalisation and mitigate overfitting, the following stochastic augmentation pipeline is applied during training. All geometric transformations are applied identically to both the image and its corresponding density map to preserve pixel-level correspondence:
1. Random horizontal flip (probability 0.5), applied consistently to image and density map.
2. Random 384×384 patch crop from a 512×512 rescaled image (probability 0.3).
3. Photometric perturbation: brightness perturbation (±20%), contrast perturbation (±20%), saturation perturbation (±20%), and hue perturbation.

**Stage 3: Normalisation:**

All images are normalised using ImageNet channel statistics (mean: [0.485, 0.456, 0.406]; standard deviation: [0.229, 0.224, 0.225] for R, G, B channels respectively). This normalisation scheme ensures that the distributions of model inputs are consistent with the weight distributions of the pretrained MobileNetV2 backbones used in both the MobileCount encoder and the routing classifier, facilitating rapid convergence and effective feature transfer.

---

