### 4.1.5 Phase 5: System Integration and Evaluation

The final phase integrates all three deployed components — the routing classifier, LCDNet, and the knowledge-distilled MobileCount — into a unified inference pipeline and evaluates the resulting system against established benchmarks and standard performance criteria.

**Performance Measures:**

The following standard crowd counting metrics are used to measure model performance throughout:

- **Mean Absolute Error (MAE):** The mean absolute difference between the predicted count and the ground-truth count across all evaluation images. MAE provides an easily interpretable measure of average counting error.
- **Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE):** The square root of the mean squared counting error. RMSE penalises large individual predictions more heavily than MAE, providing a measure of error variance and the prevalence of outlier predictions.
- **Inference Latency:** Per-image inference time in milliseconds, measured on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3050 after 30 GPU warmup passes and averaged over 30 subsequent evaluation runs.
- **Routing Accuracy:** The percentage of correctly classified images (sparse vs. dense, as defined by ground-truth count vs. T = 100) on the NWPU-Crowd validation set.
- **95% Bootstrap Confidence Intervals:** Constructed from 2,000-iteration bootstrap resampling on the 500-image validation set to provide statistically grounded uncertainty estimates for MAE.

**Integration Architecture:**

Based on the system design established in Phase 2, all three components are assembled in the HybridDensityEstimator class. At runtime, each input image is first forwarded to the routing classifier at 224×224 resolution, producing class probabilities [P(sparse), P(dense)]. If P(dense) ≥ p*, the image is forwarded to the MobileCount specialist at 384×384 resolution; otherwise to LCDNet at 384×384 resolution. The selected model generates a density map from which the crowd count is derived by spatial summation. Following training, the routing threshold p* is optimised by a post-hoc sweep over ten candidate values on the validation set, identifying p* = 0.85 as the optimal operating point.

---

## 4.2 Preliminary Design and Model Specification

### 4.2.1 Dataset Characteristics

Dataset selection for crowd density estimation requires careful consideration of annotation quality, scene diversity, density variance, and applicability to real-world deployment scenarios. Two complementary datasets are employed in this work: NWPU-Crowd as the primary training and evaluation benchmark, and the ShanghaiTech dataset for cross-dataset generalisation evaluation. The statistical characteristics of these datasets are summarised in Table 4.1.

NWPU-Crowd presents extreme diversity in crowd density, encompassing scenes from entirely empty frames (count = 0) to highly congested events with over 20,000 individuals. This breadth makes it uniquely suitable for training and evaluating all three deployed components across the full operating spectrum of the routing system. ShanghaiTech Part A provides moderately dense to very dense scenes with precise point-level annotations, while ShanghaiTech Part B provides predominantly sparse street-level scenes. The complementary density profiles of these datasets enable robust evaluation of generalisation across density distributions.

