### 4.2.2 LCDNet Architecture Specification

**Design Philosophy:**

LCDNet was designed with an explicit focus on parameter efficiency, using depthwise separable convolutions to achieve an approximately 8× reduction in learned parameters relative to standard convolutional architectures of equivalent depth, while maintaining sufficient representational capacity for accurate density estimation in sparse-to-medium crowd conditions. The design is intended to operate as the fast path of the hybrid system and must provide accurate predictions for scenes with fewer than 100 people.

**Architecture Overview:**

LCDNet employs an encoder-decoder architecture with symmetric skip connections that support information flow between spatially co-located encoder and decoder stages. The encoder applies four successive downsampling operations, progressively increasing channel depth to capture hierarchical features ranging from low-level edges and textures to high-level semantic patterns. Each encoder block contains two depthwise separable convolution (DSConv) layers — each comprising a depthwise convolution followed by a pointwise (1×1) convolution, batch normalisation, and ReLU activation — followed by MaxPool2×2 downsampling. The final encoder stage (Stage 4, bottleneck) does not apply pooling. Each decoder stage applies bilinear interpolation upsampling, concatenates the upsampled features with the skip connection from the corresponding encoder stage, and applies two DSConv layers to refine the features. The output head converts the final feature map into a single-channel density map via a 3×3 convolution followed by a 1×1 convolution and ReLU activation, ensuring non-negative density values.

The detailed layer-wise specification is provided in Table 4.2.

---

**[Table 4.2 — LCDNet Detailed Layer-wise Specification]**

| Component | Layer Configuration | Dimensionality Transform | Parameters | Functional Role |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Encoder Stage 1** | DSConv×2 + MaxPool2×2 | 384×384×3 → 192×192×64 | ~3,500 | Primary feature extraction |
| **Encoder Stage 2** | DSConv×2 + MaxPool2×2 | 192×192×64 → 96×96×128 | ~17,000 | Intermediate representations |
| **Encoder Stage 3** | DSConv×2 + MaxPool2×2 | 96×96×128 → 48×48×256 | ~66,000 | Mid-level semantic features |
| **Encoder Stage 4 (Bottleneck)** | DSConv×2 (no pool) | 48×48×256 → 48×48×512 | ~264,000 | High-level abstractions |
| Dropout2D (p=0.2) | Regularisation | 48×48×512 | 0 | Prevent overfitting |
| **Decoder Stage 1** | Upsample + Concat + DSConv×2 | 48×48×512 → 96×96×256 | ~396,000 | Initial spatial reconstruction |
| **Decoder Stage 2** | Upsample + Concat + DSConv×2 | 96×96×256 → 192×192×128 | ~99,000 | Intermediate upsampling |
| **Decoder Stage 3** | Upsample + Concat + DSConv×2 | 192×192×128 → 384×384×64 | ~25,000 | Fine-grained reconstruction |
| **Output Head** | Conv3×3(64→32) + ReLU + Conv1×1(32→1) + ReLU | 384×384×64 → 384×384×1 | ~18,500 | Density map generation |
| **Total** | | | **≈0.917M** | Full-resolution output |

*DSConv = Depthwise Conv + Pointwise Conv + BN + ReLU. Skip connections concatenate encoder feature maps (before pooling) with upsampled decoder features at each resolution.*

