### 4.2.4 Routing Classifier Specification

**Design Philosophy:**

The routing classifier achieves effective scene complexity assessment via transfer learning, utilising the pretrained MobileNetV2 backbone's visual representations to perform rapid and accurate binary labelling with minimal computational overhead. By operating on the full visual content of the input image rather than hand-crafted proxies such as pixel intensity, the classifier learns to assess scene density from structural, textural, and contextual cues simultaneously.

**Architecture:**

The routing classifier uses the full MobileNetV2 backbone (pretrained on ImageNet) as the feature extractor, replacing the original classification head with a custom two-class head. After the backbone's adaptive average pooling layer produces a 1280-dimensional feature vector, the classification head is:

Dropout(p=0.3) → Linear(1280 → 256) → ReLU → Dropout(p=0.3) → Linear(256 → 2)

The 2-class logits are converted to routing probabilities by softmax: [P(sparse), P(dense)]. The routing decision is: route to MobileCount if P(dense) ≥ p*; route to LCDNet if P(dense) < p*.

The architecture specification is summarised in Table 4.5.

---

**[Table 4.5 — Routing Classifier Architecture Specification]**

| Component | Type | Dimensionality | Parameters | Training Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| MobileNetV2 Backbone | Feature extractor | 224×224×3 → 1280 | 2,257,984 | Fine-tuned (pretrained init) |
| Global Average Pooling | Spatial reduction | 1280 | 0 | — |
| Dropout Layer | Regularisation | 1280 | 0 | p = 0.3 |
| Linear Layer 1 | Fully connected | 1280 → 256 | 327,936 | Trainable |
| ReLU | Activation | 256 | 0 | — |
| Dropout Layer 2 | Regularisation | 256 | 0 | p = 0.3 |
| Linear Layer 2 (Head) | Fully connected | 256 → 2 | 514 | Trainable |
| **Total** | | | **~2.552M** | |

*Routing label: sparse (0) if GT count ≤ 100; dense (1) if GT count > 100. Threshold T = 100.*

---

### 4.2.5 Preprocessing Specifications

**Density Map Synthesis:**

Ground-truth density maps are synthesised by placing adaptive Gaussian kernels at annotated head positions. The standard deviation σᵢ of the kernel centred at head position i is given by:

σᵢ = β · d̄ᵢ

where d̄ᵢ = (1/3) · Σⱼ₌₁³ ‖pᵢ − pⱼ‖₂ is the mean Euclidean distance from annotation i to its three nearest neighbours, and β = 0.3 is the empirically established scaling coefficient. This adaptive formulation produces narrower kernels in congested regions (improving localisation precision) and wider kernels in sparse regions (providing adequate spatial coverage). The result is a density map D(x,y) satisfying mass conservation:

∫∫ D(x, y) dxdy = N

where N is the total number of annotated head positions in the image, enabling pixel-wise regression under a supervised learning framework.

